  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How do I describe a character?",
      "a": "Show four things: how they look, what they say, what they do and what they think. Actions and words often tell more than looks.",
      "asks": [
        "Describing a character"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I show a character through speech?",
      "a": "Give them words that fit who they are. A shy character might whisper, \"Um, can I play?\" A bossy one might say, \"Give me that!\"",
      "asks": [
        "Character dialogue tips"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a setting in a story?",
      "a": "The setting is where and when a story happens, like a busy market in Lagos at sunrise or a spaceship in the year 3000.",
      "asks": [
        "Setting meaning"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I make a setting come alive?",
      "a": "Use your senses: what you can see, hear, smell, touch and even taste there. Pick a few strong details rather than a long list.",
      "asks": [
        "Describe a setting with senses"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does show, don't tell mean?",
      "a": "Instead of telling the reader Zainab was kind, show it: Zainab shared her last biscuit. Readers enjoy working it out.",
      "asks": [
        "Show not tell"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The main character in a story is often called the protagonist.",
    "Smell is strongly linked to memory, so a smell detail can make a setting feel very real."
  ],
